{"task":"Collapse the three serving modes into one","goals":"One serving mode that works on every setup, with per-site servers as an escalation rather than an install-wide choice","status":"Questions","recorded_at":"2026-09-09T15:30:26Z","body":"ldev had three serving modes, and the choice between them was charged to the whole\ninstall rather than to the sites that actually needed it.\n\nOnly one of the three did what the tool promises. \"auto\" owns 80 and 443 for the\nwhole TLD, so a new directory is live immediately and a hostname with nothing behind\nit lands on the dashboard. \"persite\" gave up those ports, and with them the fallback,\nthe portless URLs and the .localhost origins — an unknown name reached a connection\nerror instead of a page, which read as a bug in the tool rather than as the mode\nworking exactly as designed. \"apache\" never worked at all: install.sh rendered\ntemplates/httpd-vhosts.tmpl, and that file has never existed in this repo, so picking\nit killed the installer under `set -e` before the config was written.\n\nThe four things docs/persite.md justified the mode with — a different PHP version,\nits own certificate, a proxy to a running app, restarts that leave the neighbours\nalone — are all properties of ONE site. None of them needs the other sites to give up\nport 443, and none of them needs a second serving model. They are now a per-site\nescalation instead: `ldev standalone <name>` writes that site its own Caddyfile on a\nhigh port, and the wildcard server fronts it with a generated reverse_proxy block. A\nsite that takes this route keeps the portless URL, the certificate and the fallback,\nnone of which the old mode could give it.\n\nWhat this deletes:\n\n  - the MODE question, and MODE from the saved config\n  - apache mode entirely, along with a mkcert dependency nothing called any more\n  - the branching on MODE in doctor, apply, restart, status and new\n\nTwo collisions that only existed because the modes were separate are fixed with it.\n\nSite admin ports used to start at ADMIN_PORT — the same value the wildcard server\nbinds — so the first standalone site was handed a port the front door already held,\nand a Caddy that cannot bind its admin API exits rather than warning. The site was\nsimply never up. Allocation now starts one above it and treats both of the wildcard\nserver's ports as claimed.\n\nA site Caddyfile naming port 80 or 443 would generate a block proxying that hostname\nto the very server doing the proxying. Apply now refuses, names the file and serves\nthat host from disk. This is not hypothetical: a Caddy address line gives each host\non it its own address, so ~/Sites/matsu.ldev's `matsu.ldev, matsu-dev.ldev:8444 {`\nputs matsu.ldev on the default 443 — which is also why that site never started.\n\nVerified against the real ~/Sites: the generator detects both legacy HTTPS sites and\nnew plain-HTTP ones and dials each correctly, skips the port-443 clash with a warning,\nand the fully assembled config passes `caddy validate`. 34 tests pass, up from 16 —\nthe new ones cover bare directory names as siblings, the admin-port floor, proxy block\ngeneration, and the proxy-loop refusal.\n\n`ldev render` is split out of `ldev apply` because install.sh needs the config on disk\nbefore it can bootstrap the daemon that reads it, and apply ends by restarting a\nservice that does not exist yet at that point.\n\nNot done here: WordPress sites still pin WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL to their old :8443 URL and\nredirect back to it when fronted. That is site data rather than ldev's own files, so\nit needs a decision before anything touches it.\n","parent_commit":"e46b66f92511ab2e20d21d5c36ffd56f74291fa9","repo":"brave-cedar-fb33","pct":"92","tasks":[{"id":"diagnose","content":"Diagnose sites.ldev: persite mode, nothing on 80/443, no wildcard fallback","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"bare-dirs","content":"Serve bare directory names in the wildcard template","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"list","content":"Make ldev list report bare dirs and flag unreachable names","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"mode","content":"Decide how to change this machine: consolidate to one mode","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"consolidate","content":"Consolidate the three modes into one","status":"completed","children":[{"id":"proxy-front","content":"Wildcard server fronts a site running its own server, verified against live seasonal-drops:8443","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"apply-gen","content":"ldev render/apply generates a proxy block per standalone site","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"admin-port","content":"Site admin ports start above the wildcard server's, which it used to collide with","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"loop-guard","content":"Refuse to front a site claiming port 80 or 443, which would proxy to this server","status":"completed"},{"id":"drop-modes","content":"Remove the install-time mode question; per-site becomes ldev standalone","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"apache","content":"Drop apache mode, whose template was never committed","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"}]},{"id":"docs","content":"Rewrite README serving-modes section and docs/standalone-sites.md","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"wp-urls","content":"WordPress sites pin WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL to :8443 and redirect back when fronted","status":"pending","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"}],"questions":[{"id":"q2","prompt":"Your WordPress sites pin WP_HOME/WP_SIT